[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) Consuming polyphenol-rich foods: Polyphenols can exert prebiotic effects by positively influencing gut bacteria, possibly promoting the growth of SCFA-producing bacteria. However, their specific effect on butyric acid is unclear.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) Administering LPS from gram-negative bacteria: Lipopolysaccharides can act as a potent stimulator of the gut immune system. While they may influence the gut microbiome, their direct role in increasing butyric acid-producing bacteria is not well-established.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) Utilizing mushroom-derived polysaccharides alone: Polysaccharides from mushrooms can act as prebiotics, supporting the growth of certain gut bacteria. They may promote SCFA production, but their specific effect on butyric acid is not certain.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Increasing oligosaccharide-based prebiotics only: Oligosaccharides are known to selectively promote the growth of specific beneficial bacteria, including some that produce butyric acid. This targeted approach can lead to an increased population of butyric acid-producing bacteria.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
