[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which property of nitric oxide (NO) most directly explains its ability to induce the soxRS response in E. coli under conditions where extracellular superoxide cannot?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) Its ability to generate ATP through oxidative phosphorylation   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) Its charged nature, promoting membrane association   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) Its rapid enzymatic degradation by catalase   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) Its uncharged and nonpolar character, allowing membrane diffusion   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) Its role as a cofactor for glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Its covalent modification of DNA bases   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) Its specificity for activating the lac operon
