[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Alright, so I need to figure out which toxin produced by Clostridium perfringens is now recognized as essential for inducing necrotic enteritis in broiler chickens. The question mentions that this shifts the previous focus from another toxin. 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m First, I remember that C. perfringens is a common cause of various types of enteritis and enterotoxemia in animals, especially in poultry. It produces several toxins, which are classified based on their electrophoretic mobility under denaturing gel electrophoresis (DE). These toxins are named alpha, beta, epsilon, zeta, etc.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Necrotic enteritis in broilers is often associated with C. perfringens. In the past, I think the primary toxin linked to this disease was the beta toxin. But the question says that now the focus has shifted to another toxin, meaning that the essential toxin for necrotic enteritis might not be beta anymore.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Wait, let me think again. I recall that there was a shift in understanding regarding which toxin is responsible. One of the newer toxins identified is NetB. NetB is a new type of toxin produced by C. perfringens type A. Necrotic enteritis in chickens is often caused by C. perfringens type A, and the NetB toxin has been implicated in this disease.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Previously, the beta toxin was the main focus, but now NetB is recognized as the primary toxin responsible for necrotic enteritis. The shift happened because NetB is more strongly associated with the disease than beta toxin. 
