[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5. Genes involved in phosphorus uptake: These are specific to certain metabolic functions and might be less conserved.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6. Carbohydrate-active enzyme genes: These could be functionally important but may not be as highly conserved.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7. Genes unique to horizontal gene transfer: These are often novel but may not skew coverage as much as highly conserved sequences.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m The most likely features masked would be the highly conserved ones. Ribosomal RNA and tRNA genes are typically among the most conserved sequences in genomes. Overabundance of these would skew metagenomic coverage because they might come from a wide range of sources, including both the target bacterium and other microbes, leading to an inaccurate representation of the community composition.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Therefore, option 2, which refers to rRNA and tRNA genes, is the best answer because these genes are highly conserved and their overrepresentation can mask other features, skewing metagenomic coverage.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m </think>
