[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m Open data sharing and clearly defined contributor roles have become essential practices in computational biology, enabling transparency, reproducibility, and collaborative research. These standards are particularly important in large-scale projects involving metabolic modeling and industrial biotechnology applications.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which combination of practices best demonstrates a commitment to transparency, reproducibility, and open science in computational biology research?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) Making both computational models and analysis code publicly available along with a detailed breakdown of individual author contributions   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) Publishing research findings in a closed-access journal and restricting code access to collaborators   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) Providing only summary results without sharing underlying data or code   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) Listing all authors alphabetically without specifying individual roles or responsibilities   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) Depositing experimental data in a proprietary database with limited access   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Citing previous work without offering any new tools or open resources   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) Releasing only the final manuscript without supplementary materials or contributor information
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
