[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m Arthrobacter species are soil-dwelling bacteria that inhabit a variety of environments, including the extreme cold of Antarctic soils and more temperate climates. Comparative genomics can reveal how these bacteria adapt to different environmental stresses through changes in genome structure and gene content.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which genome feature is most consistently associated with Antarctic Arthrobacter strains compared to temperate strains and is likely an adaptation to their specialized, cold environment?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) Larger genome size   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) Increased number of horizontally transferred genes above 10%   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) Lower maximum temperature for growth   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) Higher percentage of non-coding DNA   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) Elevated number of rRNA genes   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Broader temperature growth range   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) Dramatically lower G+C content relative to temperate strains
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
