[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Wait, higher G+C content increases the melting temperature of DNA. So, if the environment is cold, the DNA might need a higher melting temperature to remain stable. Therefore, lower G+C content would mean a lower melting temperature, which might not be good in cold environments because the DNA would denature more easily. So, maybe the opposite: higher G+C content is better in cold environments because it keeps the DNA stable. Therefore, if temperate strains have higher G+C, but Antarctic strains might have lower. So, the G+C content is lower in Antarctic strains, which makes their DNA more stable at lower temperatures. That seems like a possible adaptation.
