[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m The selection of lactic acid bacteria (LAB) strains as probiotics for poultry and humans depends on their ability to survive harsh gastrointestinal conditions and their antibiotic susceptibility profiles. Important criteria include tolerance to bile salts, resistance or sensitivity to specific antibiotics, and functional properties such as enzyme inhibition.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which combination of characteristics is most critical for a LAB strain to be considered a safe and effective probiotic candidate for gut health improvement in poultry and humans?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) High resistance to penicillin, inability to survive at pH 2.0, and sensitivity to ceftriaxone   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) Sensitivity to vancomycin and erythromycin, tolerance to 0.3% bile salts, and survival at pH 2.0   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) Tolerance to 1% bile salts, resistance to all tested antibiotics, and α-amylase inhibition   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) Sensitivity to novobiocin, no α-glucosidase activity, and low acid tolerance   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) High resistance to streptomycin, poor bile salt tolerance, and survival only at neutral pH   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Sensitivity to gentamicin, inability to inhibit α-glucosidase, and resistance to acidic environments   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) Resistance to ciprofloxacin, high bile salt tolerance, and failure to survive at pH 2.0
