[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m To determine which strategy directly combines targeted antimicrobial activity and specific titanium-binding capability to inhibit biofilm formation on titanium implants, we analyze the given options:
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) **Hydrophobic polycationic polymer coatings**: These coatings may have antimicrobial properties but do not specifically bind to titanium effectively.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) **Plant-synthesized metallic nanoparticles**: While nanoparticles can have antimicrobial effects, they may not specifically bind to titanium surfaces.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) **Totarol-based natural antibacterial coatings**: Totarol is an antimicrobial agent, but the effectiveness in combining with titanium binding is unclear.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) **Rhamnolipid biosurfactant coatings**: Biosurfactants can reduce surface tension and inhibit microbial growth but may not specifically bind to titanium.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) **Engineered chimeric peptides with antimicrobial and titanium-binding functions**: Chimeric peptides can be designed to have both antimicrobial activity and specific binding to titanium, addressing both requirements effectively.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) **Bismuth-doped calcium phosphate cement**: This material may have antimicrobial properties but does not specifically bind to titanium.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) **F-doped TiO2 microporous coatings**: These coatings can enhance antimicrobial activity but may not specifically bind to titanium.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m The correct strategy is option 5, as engineered chimeric peptides can combine both targeted antimicrobial activity and specific titanium-binding functions to inhibit biofilm formation.
