[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m The bacterial stringent response is a highly conserved adaptation mechanism that enables cells to survive under nutrient limitation and stress by regulating various aspects of cell physiology. Mutations affecting ribosomal components or alarmone synthesis can profoundly alter this response and impact bacterial survival, morphology, and metabolism.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which of the following mutations would most directly abolish (p)ppGpp synthesis in response to uncharged tRNA accumulation, leading to a "relaxed" phenotype during amino acid starvation?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) Deletion of the relA gene encoding the ribosome-associated (p)ppGpp synthetase   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) Overexpression of ribosomal protein S12   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) Inactivation of the rpoB gene encoding the β subunit of RNA polymerase   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) Mutation in the lacZ gene involved in lactose metabolism   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) Loss-of-function mutation in the spoT gene's hydrolase domain   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Alteration of the dnaA gene involved in DNA replication initiation   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) Deletion of an efflux pump gene conferring antibiotic resistance
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
