[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which of the following best explains why Pseudomonas aeruginosa can effectively survive in environments with fluctuating oxidative stress?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) It lacks superoxide dismutase but has enhanced DNA repair enzymes.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) It produces a single catalase enzyme with broad specificity.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) Its oxidative stress defense genes are constitutively expressed at all times.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) It possesses multiple isoforms of antioxidant enzymes and regulates their expression in response to oxidative conditions.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) It relies exclusively on iron sequestration to prevent hydroxyl radical formation.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) It uses SoxR to regulate catalase expression in response to hydrogen peroxide.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) It depends only on free radical scavengers for protection against ROIs.
