[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m Titanium dioxide (TiO₂) is widely used in photocatalysis, with its performance influenced by crystalline phase, dopant incorporation, and calcination temperature. Doping TiO₂ with transition metals like copper can tailor its electronic structure and enhance antimicrobial activity under visible light.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which of the following outcomes results from copper doping in TiO₂ when calcined at temperatures up to 650°C, as compared to pure anatase TiO₂?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) Complete transformation of anatase to rutile phase at 600°C   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) Increase of bandgap from 3.2 eV to 3.6 eV   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) No significant change in visible light absorption   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) Stabilization of anatase phase at higher temperatures   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) Decrease in photocatalytic activity against both Gram-negative and Gram-positive bacteria   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Formation of a new crystalline phase unrelated to anatase or rutile   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) Elimination of oxygen vacancies in the crystal lattice
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
