[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Wait, another thought: I remember that some bacteria have proteins or structures on their surface that allow them to bind to cells. These could be related to hydrophobic interactions or specific adhesion molecules. Cell surface hydrophobicity might make it easier for LAB to stick to the epithelial cells by avoiding water interactions and having a more sticky surface.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m So, putting it all together, the property that most directly relates to adhesion is probably high cell surface hydrophobicity because it could prevent the LAB from being washed away and help them stick more effectively to the cells in the gut.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m </think>
