[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m The gut microbiome is a diverse ecosystem of microorganisms in the digestive tract, playing a key role in human health. Mathematical models are used to understand how disturbances like antibiotics can cause lasting changes in its composition and stability.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which feature of high-diversity ecological systems, such as the gut microbiome, explains why disturbances may shift them into different stable community states rather than returning them to their original composition?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) Reduced interspecies competition due to antibiotic resistance genes   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) The presence of a single dominant bacterial species controlling stability   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) Emergence of alternative stable states from complex species interactions   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) Inability of Bayesian models to account for uncertainty in recovery   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) Uniform recovery rates of all bacterial species after perturbation   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Linear dynamics governing all microbial population changes   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) Absence of nutrient utilization diversity among microbial taxa
