[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m The gut microbiome of chickens has been characterized at a large scale using high-throughput metagenomics, enabling detailed comparisons with the gut gene catalogs of other animals such as humans and pigs. Understanding the uniqueness and overlap of microbial genes among these species can inform strategies for animal health and cross-species interventions.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which of the following statements most accurately describes the overlap of gut microbial genes among chickens, pigs, and humans?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) Over 50% of gut microbial genes are shared among all three species.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) Chickens and pigs share more gut microbial genes with each other than with humans.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) About 10% of gut microbial genes are shared between chickens and humans, and between pigs and humans, but only about 0.8% between chickens and pigs.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) The majority of chicken gut microbial genes belong to archaea and eukaryotes.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) Firmicutes and Bacteroidetes are absent from the chicken gut gene catalog.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) More than 80% of microbial genes are shared by chickens, pigs, and humans.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) Only pig gut microbial genes are functionally annotated in public databases.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
