[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m DNA double-strand break (DSB) repair is essential for preserving genome integrity in mammalian cells, with several protein complexes orchestrating detection and repair. Viruses can manipulate host DNA repair mechanisms to favor their own replication, impacting both viral pathogenesis and cellular responses.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which protein complex is recruited to sites of DNA double-strand breaks via recognition of gamma-H2AX, and what domain in this complex is primarily responsible for this recruitment?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) Ku70/80 complex; leucine zipper domain   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) MRN (Mre11-Rad50-NBS1) complex; FHA and BRCT domains in NBS1   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) BRCA1-BARD1 complex; RING finger domain   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) DNA-PKcs; HEAT repeat domain   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) XRCC4-Ligase IV complex; coiled-coil domain in XRCC4   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) PARP1 complex; zinc finger domain   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) 53BP1 complex; Tudor domain
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
