[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m Nanotechnology-based delivery systems are increasingly used to enhance the efficacy and safety of oral antimicrobial therapies. Key advances focus on improving drug targeting, stability, and minimizing disruption to the gut microbiome.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which property of silver nanoparticles (AgNPs) distinguishes them from traditional broad-spectrum antibiotics in the context of oral antimicrobial therapy?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) They are completely inert and never interact with beneficial gut bacteria.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) They always selectively kill only pathogenic microorganisms.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) They offer high specificity, rapid antimicrobial action, and minimal impact on the native gut microbiome.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) They must be administered intravenously to be effective.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) They are rapidly degraded in the gastrointestinal tract, limiting their usefulness for oral delivery.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) They cannot be engineered for targeted delivery or altered surface properties.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) They invariably lead to significant dysbiosis of gut microbiota after oral administration.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
