[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m Arthrobacter species are resilient actinobacteria that thrive in extreme environments, such as Antarctic soils. Genomic analyses provide insight into their adaptation mechanisms at both structural and functional levels.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which feature distinguishes Antarctic Arthrobacter strains from their temperate counterparts and is likely an adaptation to slow growth rates in cold environments?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) Higher G+C content in the genome   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) Increased number of open reading frames (ORFs)   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) Greater abundance of horizontally transferred genes   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) Significantly fewer copies of the 16S rRNA gene   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) Presence of unique phage integrase genes   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Higher coding density in the genome   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) Predominance of carbohydrate metabolism genes
