[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m Prochlorococcus is a globally abundant marine cyanobacterium whose cell cycle is intricately linked to light exposure and internal timing mechanisms. Understanding the regulation of its cell division has important implications for marine ecology and primary production.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which statement most accurately characterizes the regulation of cell cycle events in Prochlorococcus populations in oceanic surface waters?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) DNA replication and cell division are triggered solely by the intensity of light at any given moment.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) Cell division occurs continuously throughout both light and dark periods without synchronization.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) Newly born cells are capable of dividing in complete darkness if nutrients are sufficient.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) A light-triggered timer or circadian clock, rather than just light intensity or duration, governs the timing of cell cycle events.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) The G1 phase in all Prochlorococcus cells is prolonged to maximize DNA repair before division.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Prochlorococcus shares an identical division pattern with coccolithophores and diatoms.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) Only one round of cell division per 24-hour period is possible, regardless of light conditions.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
