[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m Antimicrobial coatings for dental implants are being developed to reduce the risk of infection and improve implant integration. The effectiveness of these coatings depends on their antibacterial properties, biocompatibility, and relevance to the specific microbial environment of the oral cavity.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which of the following statements most accurately reflects current challenges in translating in vitro findings about antibiotic-coated titanium dental implants to clinical dental practice?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) Gentamicin and vancomycin coatings effectively target the most common anaerobic bacteria in the oral cavity.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) Most in vitro studies use complex multi-species biofilm models that closely mimic oral conditions.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) Vancomycin and gentamicin are the preferred antibiotics for treating dental implant infections in vivo.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) The oral cavity’s dynamic environment, including saliva, pH fluctuations, and diverse biofilms, limits the direct clinical applicability of laboratory results.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) All antibiotic coatings that perform well in vitro are safe and effective for use in patients.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Titanium nitride coatings have a universally consistent antimicrobial effect across all bacterial strains.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) Chlorhexidine is rarely considered for implant coatings due to its lack of antimicrobial substantivity.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
