[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m DNA base excision repair is essential for correcting oxidative lesions in bacterial genomes, influencing both antibiotic resistance and survival under oxidative stress. In Campylobacter jejuni, specific gene mutations can alter the regulation of oxidative stress response pathways and affect multiple phenotypes.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which of the following most directly explains how the loss of Nth (endonuclease III) function in Campylobacter jejuni leads to both chloramphenicol resistance and increased oxidative stress resistance?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) Accumulation of thymine glycol lesions causes G:C to A:T transitions in the perR gene, resulting in derepression of PerR-controlled oxidative stress response genes   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) Loss of Nth enhances the expression of antibiotic efflux pumps independently of DNA repair pathways   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) Nth deficiency leads to increased repair of oxidized guanine, promoting resistance phenotypes   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) The absence of Nth activates Cj1083c, which compensates by excising all oxidative lesions   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) perR gene mutations directly inhibit antibiotic uptake proteins, reducing chloramphenicol sensitivity   
