[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which statement best describes how increasing ethanol concentrations (up to 12% v/v) affect the energy requirements and ATP generation pathways of Oenococcus oeni during growth?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) Higher ethanol concentrations decrease the demand for non-growth associated maintenance energy (NGAM) and reduce ATP synthesis via F0F1-ATPase.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) At ethanol concentrations of 9% and above, NGAM requirements are dramatically elevated, with F0F1-ATPase generating up to 80% of the required ATP.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) Increasing ethanol levels stimulate malolactic fermentation exclusively as the main ATP-generating pathway, replacing F0F1-ATPase function.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) NGAM requirements remain constant regardless of ethanol concentration due to efficient membrane adaptations in O. oeni.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) Low ethanol concentrations (3–6%) sharply impair nutrient exchange and decrease growth rate compared to higher ethanol concentrations.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Membrane lipid composition changes only at ethanol concentrations below 3%, resulting in decreased stress tolerance.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) The presence of ethanol at any concentration inhibits both ATP production and proton extrusion in O. oeni.
