[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m Essential oils (EOs) are volatile aromatic compounds derived from various plant parts and are widely studied for their antimicrobial properties. Their effectiveness can vary greatly depending on plant species, chemical composition, and interactions between different oils.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which of the following statements accurately reflects current evidence regarding the antibacterial activity and interactions of essential oils?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) Combining thyme essential oil with parsley, lovage, or basil essential oils enhances thyme's antibacterial effectiveness.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) Sage essential oil consistently demonstrates strong antibacterial properties against a broad spectrum of bacteria.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) Only Origanum dictamnus among tested Origanum species shows notable antibacterial activity.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) Basil, chamomile blue, oregano, thyme, and tea tree essential oils are highly effective against multidrug-resistant bacteria such as Acinetobacter baumannii.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) Parsley and lovage essential oils exhibit strong antibacterial effects against both Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Oregano essential oil is universally more potent than thyme essential oil due to its phenolic content.   
