[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which of the following statements best explains why Crabtree-negative yeasts such as Komagataella phaffii and Ogataea polymorpha maintain higher cell viability and lower maintenance requirements compared to Saccharomyces cerevisiae during prolonged nutrient limitation in retentostat cultures?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) They possess specialized fermentative pathways that increase ethanol production under nutrient stress.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) Their metabolic strategies reduce reliance on fermentation, minimizing the accumulation of toxic byproducts.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) They have a higher capacity for methanol assimilation, leading to continuous cell proliferation.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) Their genomes encode additional ribosomal RNA operons that enhance protein synthesis under limitation.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) They exhibit increased rates of non-viable biomass accumulation due to ammonium limitation.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Their maintenance energy requirements are elevated by the Crabtree effect, improving stress tolerance.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) They depend primarily on anaerobic metabolism, conserving substrate for growth.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Please provide your reasoning within <think></think> tags and the final answer within a \boxed{} block. The value inside the \boxed{} block should only be the number of the correct option. So, looking at the options, the question is about why these yeasts have higher cell viability and lower maintenance during prolonged nutrient limitation. The options are 1 to 7.
