[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m Essential oils, complex mixtures derived from plants, are widely studied for their antimicrobial, antioxidant, and immunomodulatory properties. Recent systematic reviews have assessed the quality of evidence supporting these bioactivities, with particular attention to study design and methodological rigor.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which of the following statements most accurately reflects a major limitation in the current preclinical research on essential oils' biological effects?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) The majority of studies rely exclusively on human intervention trials for efficacy data.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) Most studies utilize only qualitative assays such as agar diffusion without any controls.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) The research predominantly consists of cell and animal models, with few studies involving human samples.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) All studies consistently use the same concentration ranges and incubation times for antimicrobial assays.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) Essential oils have demonstrated clinical efficacy against biofilm-associated infections in large human cohorts.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Antioxidant studies frequently omit standard assays like DPPH and ABTS.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) Immunomodulatory effects are primarily studied through randomized controlled trials in humans.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
