[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m Drought stress poses a major challenge for soil bacteria that form symbiotic relationships with legumes, such as Bradyrhizobium japonicum, impacting agricultural productivity. Understanding the cellular and molecular adaptations these bacteria use to survive desiccation is crucial for improving crop yields in arid environments.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which adaptation is most directly responsible for stabilizing proteins and cellular membranes in Bradyrhizobium japonicum during desiccation stress?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) Increased exopolysaccharide synthesis   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) Enhanced trehalose production via upregulation of otsA, otsB, and treS genes   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) Induction of DNA repair and modification enzymes   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) Activation of heat shock protein expression   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) Upregulation of pili and flagella biosynthesis   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Increased transport of osmoprotectants other than trehalose   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) Enhanced oxidative stress response protein expression
