[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m Bacterial immune systems, such as CBASS and CRISPR, use diverse signaling domains and effector proteins to defend against phage infections. The structural arrangement and activation mechanisms of these effectors are key to their function in initiating cell suicide or adaptive immunity.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which statement most accurately distinguishes between Type I and Type II Cap4 proteins in terms of their cyclic oligonucleotide (cOs) binding within the SAVED domain?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) Type I binds cAAA near the N-terminal, while Type II binds cAAG centrally within the SAVED domain.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) Type I binds cAAG centrally, and Type II binds cAAA near the C-terminal of the SAVED domain.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) Type I (e.g., AbCap4) binds cAAA centrally in the SAVED domain, whereas Type II (e.g., EcCap4) binds cAAG near the N-terminal.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) Type I only binds cAAG, and Type II only binds cAAA, both at the SAVED domain’s C-terminal.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) Both Type I and II bind cAAA but differ in their requirement for oligomerization.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Type I binds v-cADPR near the N-terminal, and Type II binds cAAA centrally.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) Both Type I and II Cap4 proteins bind cAAG near the C-terminal.
