[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m Bacteria employ multiple DNA repair mechanisms to survive the damaging effects of UV-B radiation, including photoreactivation, nucleotide excision repair, and mutagenic DNA repair systems. Mutations or genetic alterations in key repair genes can dramatically influence both survival rates and mutation frequencies after UV exposure.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m In a bacterial strain lacking both photolyase (phr) and nucleotide excision repair (uvrA), which genetic intervention would most dramatically increase survival after UV-B irradiation under photoreactivating conditions, and what is a likely accompanying consequence?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) Introduction of an intact uvrA gene, restoring nucleotide excision repair and reducing mutation frequency   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) Introduction of an intact phr gene, restoring photoreactivation without affecting mutation rates   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) Expression of rulAB determinant, resulting in dramatically increased survival via error-prone repair and elevated mutation frequency   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) Addition of a recombinase gene, enabling homologous recombination repair with low mutagenesis   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) Supplementation with antioxidants, preventing DNA damage without altering mutation frequency   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Overexpression of catalase, reducing UV-induced oxidative stress and mutation frequency   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) Introduction of a DNA ligase gene, facilitating direct repair without increased mutagenesis
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
