[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m Bacterial oxidative stress responses involve intricate regulatory networks that control the expression of enzymes to detoxify reactive oxygen species. Adaptations in these systems can affect susceptibility to antibiotics and environmental stressors.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m In Mycobacterium tuberculosis, which mechanism most directly compensates for a nonfunctional OxyR transcriptional regulator in the context of isoniazid resistance and oxidative stress?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) Upregulation of ahpC in katG mutant strains   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) Increased basal expression of katG independent of OxyR   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) Enhanced activity of OxyR-regulated katB and ahpB genes   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) Overexpression of the oxyR gene via plasmid complementation   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) Suppression of catalase-peroxidase function by regulatory RNAs   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Activation of alternative sigma factors controlling peroxidase genes   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) Inhibition of cell wall permeability to hydrogen peroxide
