[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m Gram-negative bacteria possess a complex cell envelope that includes an outer membrane populated by diverse proteins with specialized functions. Advances in proteomics and bioinformatics have enabled detailed characterization and prediction of these outer membrane proteins, offering insights into bacterial physiology, pathogenicity, and adaptation.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which property is most characteristic of outer membrane proteins in Gram-negative bacteria and is crucial for their role in nutrient uptake and interaction with the host environment?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) Presence of extensive α-helical transmembrane domains   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) Localization exclusively in the inner membrane   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) β-barrel structural architecture spanning the outer membrane   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) Involvement in cytoplasmic metabolic pathways   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) Direct synthesis in the periplasmic space without signal peptides   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Lack of post-translational modifications   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) Association solely with plasmid-encoded genes
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
