[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Hmm. So the question is about the characteristic property of outer membrane proteins in Gram-negative bacteria. The options are 1 to 7.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Let me think. Gram-negative bacteria have an outer membrane. Outer membrane proteins are involved in nutrient uptake and interacting with the environment.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Looking at the options:
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) α-helical transmembrane domains: These are more typical of inner membrane proteins, like in mitochondria or chloroplasts. The outer membrane might have β-barrels.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) Exclusively inner membrane: No, outer membrane proteins are in the outer membrane, not exclusively inner.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) β-barrel: These are common in outer membranes. Like porins, which are β-barrel structures, help in letting small molecules in.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) Cytoplasmic metabolic: Probably inner membrane or cytoplasm, not outer membrane.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) Direct synthesis without signal peptides: Signal peptides are for inner membrane or export. Outer membrane might use other signals, like those for multi-protein complexes.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Lack of modifications: I think Gram-negative outer membrane proteins might have some modifications, not sure.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) Solely plasmids: Probably not; most are chromosomal.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m So the most characteristic property is β-barrel, option 3.
