[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4. Can't cause disease in animals? I thought some Rothia can be pathogens, maybe in immunocompromised people.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5. Highly resistant to all antibiotics? Not likely; some might be susceptible.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6. Gram-negative rods? No, Rothia is gram-positive, I think.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7. Found only in soil? Probably not, as some soil bacteria can infect humans.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m So looking at statement 3: "Rothia infections are typically treated with penicillin, but some strains may exhibit resistance to beta-lactam antibiotics." That makes sense because beta-lactams include penicillins, cephalosporins, etc. So if some are resistant, then some infections might not respond to penicillin.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m So the correct answer is option 3, which is number 3.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m </think>
