[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m Bacteriophages used for therapeutic applications are typically evaluated for their genetic makeup, lifecycle, and safety profile, especially when targeting antibiotic-resistant bacterial pathogens. The genome organization of these phages often reveals functional modules related to replication, structural assembly, and host cell lysis.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which genomic feature most strongly supports the conclusion that a bacteriophage like HEf13 is an obligate lytic phage and suitable for therapeutic use against Enterococcus faecalis infections?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) Presence of genes encoding holin and endolysin in the lysis module   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) High nucleotide sequence similarity to other Sap6virus phages   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) Identification of uncharacterized open reading frames (ORFs) with low amino acid identity   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) Absence of genes associated with lysogeny, such as integrases or repressors   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) Detection of methyltransferase and terminase genes involved in DNA replication   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Use of bioinformatics tools like RAST and BLASTP for genome annotation   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) Observation of unique structural genes for tail fiber proteins
