[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m Bacteria deploy multiple DNA repair pathways to survive damaging ultraviolet (UV) radiation, including both photoreactivation and nucleotide excision repair. Genetic manipulation allows researchers to dissect the contributions of individual DNA repair genes to cellular resistance against UV-induced damage.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which of the following best explains why a Pseudomonas aeruginosa strain lacking both photolyase (phr) and uvrA genes would exhibit greater UV-B sensitivity than strains lacking only one of these genes?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) Loss of both light-dependent and excision repair mechanisms eliminates major pathways for repairing UV-induced DNA damage.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) Double mutant cells produce excess reactive oxygen species that increase UV-B damage independently of DNA repair.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) The presence of both gene deletions accelerates cell division, making cells more vulnerable to UV-B.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) Both genes encode the same enzyme, so deleting both has a redundant effect.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) Absence of photolyase alone causes maximum UV-B sensitivity, so uvrA deletion has no further impact.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Disruption of both genes leads to increased expression of efflux pumps, reducing UV resistance.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) Double mutants import more UV-B-absorbing compounds, increasing susceptibility.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
