[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m Salmonella can develop adaptive responses when exposed to sodium hypochlorite (NaClO), a common disinfectant, which challenges efforts to control bacterial survival and resistance in various environments. Multiple molecular mechanisms contribute to this adaptation, affecting susceptibility to antibiotics and disinfectants.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which of the following gene expression changes in Salmonella is most directly associated with increasing the export of toxic compounds, thereby enhancing survival during NaClO exposure?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) Upregulation of Kdp-ATPase transporter genes (kdpABC)   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) Downregulation of amino acid transporter genes (aroP, brnQ, livF)   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) Upregulation of exocytosis pump genes (emrB, yceE, ydhE, ydhC)   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) Downregulation of DNA-binding transcriptional regulators (STM0682, NagC, yaiV)   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) Upregulation of extracellular polymer substance (EPS) synthesis genes   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Downregulation of genes encoding curli fibers and cellulose   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) Upregulation of potassium efflux transporter genes
