[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m In Gram-negative bacteria, the synthesis and insertion of membrane proteins involve intricate coordination between genetic elements, ribosomes, and membrane structures. The spatial organization of these processes has significant implications for bacterial physiology and pathogenicity.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which mechanism most directly explains the close association between bacterial chromosomes, ribosomes, and the cell membrane during membrane protein synthesis?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) Active transport of proteins by periplasmic chaperones   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) Transertion coupling transcription, translation, and membrane insertion   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) Vesicular trafficking of DNA to the inner membrane   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) Degradation of misfolded membrane proteins by proteases   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) Independent translation of all membrane proteins in the cytosol   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Passive diffusion of ribosomes to membrane regions   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) Secretion of proteins via the Type III secretion system
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
