[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m In microbial communities, the production of public goods such as antibiotics can influence interactions between beneficial microbes and pathogens. The effectiveness of these defenses and the coexistence of different microbial types depend on factors like resource availability, cooperative investment, and spatial structure.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which condition is most likely to promote stable coexistence of antibiotic-producing (contributor) and non-producing (cheater) microbes in a plant rhizosphere?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) Linear benefit from public goods, high mixing, and low spatial structure   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) High host resource input and strictly linear benefit from public goods   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) Absence of diminishing returns in antibiotic effectiveness   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) Localized operation of public goods with saturating (diminishing returns) benefit functions   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) Uniformly high investment in antibiotics by all community members   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Pathogen dominance at all resource input levels   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) No variation in microbial investment or spatial assortment
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
