[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) Arr is involved in the poly-ADP-ribosylation of bacterial DNA repair proteins. Wait, Arr is a mono-ADP-ribosyltransferase, so it probably does mono-ribosylation, not poly. So option 2 is incorrect.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) Arr confers resistance to rifampin by ADP-ribosylating and inactivating the antibiotic molecule. I remember reading that Arr can inactivate some antibiotics. So maybe this is correct.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) Arr is exclusively found in eukaryotic and archaebacterial species. No, because Arr is in Mycobacterium, which is a prokaryote. So option 4 is incorrect.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) Arr modifies host immune responses through extracellular secretion. I'm not sure about this. If Arr is secreted, maybe it can affect immune responses. But how? I don't recall this property.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Arr builds long chains of ADP-ribose on target proteins like eukaryotic PARPs. Since Arr is a mono-ADP-ribosyltransferase, it should add single ADP-ribose units, not long chains. So option 6 is incorrect.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) Arr is responsible for recruiting DNA polymerases to DSBs by direct binding. I don't think Arr does that. So option 7 is incorrect.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m So, the correct option is either 1, 3, or 5. Hmm.
