[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m Dematiaceous (melanized) fungi are a diverse group of pigmented molds known to cause severe infections, particularly in individuals with compromised immune systems. Advances in antifungal therapy and molecular taxonomy are shaping clinical approaches to these challenging pathogens.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which of the following statements best describes a key factor contributing to the pathogenicity of dematiaceous fungi in human hosts?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) Their ability to form biofilms on medical devices   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) The presence of melanin in their cell walls, which protects them from immune responses   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) Their exclusive growth at low temperatures   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) Their production of potent exotoxins that damage host tissue   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) Their complete resistance to all known antifungal agents   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Their rapid replication in the bloodstream   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) The lack of any environmental reservoir for these fungi
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
