[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m Disinfectants like sodium hypochlorite (NaClO) are widely used to control bacterial contamination, but their interaction with microbial cells can elicit complex molecular responses. In Salmonella Enteritidis, exposure to NaClO can lead to changes in gene expression that affect both cell membrane integrity and metabolic pathways.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which of the following best describes a dual consequence of increased membrane permeability in Salmonella Enteritidis following NaClO exposure?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) Enhanced metabolic activity and increased cell division   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) Improved nutrient uptake and higher resistance to oxidative stress   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) Facilitation of extracellular DNA uptake and potential release of antibiotic resistance genes   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) Complete eradication of bacterial populations and prevention of gene transfer   
