[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m In bioprocess engineering, mathematical models are used to describe and optimize the dynamics of microbial populations in continuous cultivation systems such as retentostats. Accurate modeling of growth, death, and maintenance metabolism is essential for predicting biomass yields and recombinant protein production.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which methodological approach ensures that a mathematical model of viable and dead biomass dynamics does not predict biologically impossible scenarios, such as dead cells spontaneously becoming viable again?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) Using a constant death rate parameter throughout the cultivation   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) Setting negative calculated death rates to zero in the model equations   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) Ignoring maintenance energy requirements in the model   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) Fitting a linear equation to viable biomass fraction over time   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) Incorporating only glucose uptake rates without viability data   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Assuming biomass composition remains fixed at all growth rates   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) Using gas exchange data without correction for instrument sensitivity
