[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m Ultraviolet-B (UV-B) radiation poses a significant threat to bacterial survival in sunlit environments by inflicting DNA damage that can disrupt vital cellular processes. Bacteria have evolved multiple DNA repair mechanisms to counteract these effects and maintain ecological fitness.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which DNA repair pathway directly reverses cyclobutane pyrimidine dimers (CPDs) in bacterial DNA using light energy harvested by photolyase enzymes?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) Nucleotide excision repair (NER)   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) Base excision repair (BER)   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) Photoreactivation   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) Mismatch repair   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) Recombinational DNA repair   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Mutagenic DNA repair (MDR)   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) Non-homologous end joining (NHEJ)
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
