[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m In certain bacteria, biofilm formation is regulated by the interplay between surface adhesins, proteases, and intracellular signaling molecules such as c-di-GMP. The Lap system, consisting of LapA, LapD, and LapG, is a key regulatory module influencing the retention or release of adhesins from the cell surface.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which of the following best describes how high intracellular c-di-GMP levels affect LapA localization and biofilm formation in bacteria possessing the Lap system?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) c-di-GMP directly increases LapG protease activity, leading to LapA removal from the cell surface and reduced biofilm formation.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) c-di-GMP binds LapD, causing LapD to inhibit LapG, which prevents LapA cleavage and promotes its retention on the cell surface, enhancing biofilm formation.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) c-di-GMP degrades LapA, resulting in decreased biofilm formation.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) c-di-GMP prevents LapD from binding LapG, allowing LapG to release LapA into the supernatant.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) c-di-GMP activates LapG gene expression, increasing LapA removal from the cell surface.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) c-di-GMP binds LapA, causing it to dissociate from the cell surface.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) c-di-GMP inhibits LapD oligomerization, thereby increasing LapG activity.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Please provide your reasoning within <think></think> tags and the final answer within a \boxed{} block. The value inside the \boxed{} block should only be the number of the correct option. So the final answer is just \boxed{2}.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Looking at the options:
