[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m In social insects such as fungus-growing ants, the composition and maintenance of the cuticular microbiome play a significant role in colony defense against pathogens. The dynamics of microbial transmission and the integration of multiple microbial species are critical factors shaping long-term microbiome stability and effectiveness.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which process most directly promotes the initial establishment of a protective, beneficial microbiome in new ant colonies when vertical transmission is absent?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) Horizontal gene transfer of antibiotic resistance genes among soil microbes   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) Behavioral defenses like grooming performed by worker ants   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) High rates of beneficial immigration from the environment   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) Long-term coexistence of multiple actinomycete species   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) Evolution of pathogen resistance to existing antibiotics   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Niche partitioning among established cuticular microbes   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) Dense colonization of ant workers by vertically transmitted bacteria
