[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m Ribosomally synthesized and posttranslationally modified peptides (RiPPs) are a diverse superfamily of natural products produced primarily by bacteria, with crucial roles in microbial ecosystems and significant potential as therapeutic agents. Their unique bioactivities and structural variety arise from specific enzymatic modifications following ribosomal biosynthesis.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which of the following best explains why RiPPs are considered promising alternatives to conventional antibiotics in the fight against bacterial resistance?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) They are exclusively produced by eukaryotic organisms and thus evade bacterial defense mechanisms.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) Their posttranslational modifications result in novel mechanisms of action that can bypass common resistance pathways.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) Their ecological role is limited to nutrient acquisition, minimizing selective pressure on pathogens.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) They lack any cytotoxic or antimicrobial properties, making them harmless to non-target organisms.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) They are only effective in plant-microbe interactions and not in human medicine.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Their biosynthesis does not involve ribosomal translation, allowing for greater structural diversity.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) They are rapidly degraded in microbial communities, reducing the risk of resistance development.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
