[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which of the following best explains the functional consequence of deleting the lexA1 gene in Xanthomonas axonopodis pv. citri, as assessed by luciferase reporter assays under DNA-damaging conditions?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) The lexA1 promoter remains repressed and shows no inducibility by DNA damage.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) The lexA1 promoter becomes constitutively active, regardless of DNA damage.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) The lexA2 promoter compensates by repressing the lexA1 promoter in the absence of LexA1.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) Both lexA1 and lexA2 promoters become unresponsive to DNA damage.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) The recA promoter activity is abolished due to lack of LexA1.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) LexA2 increases its binding affinity for the lexA1 promoter and represses it effectively.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) Non-specific competitor DNA prevents any activation of the lexA1 promoter.
