[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m Bacterial biofilm formation and dispersal are tightly regulated processes, often controlled by environmental signals and intracellular second messengers such as c-di-GMP. Proteins involved in these regulatory networks can change their localization and activity in response to specific cues, impacting biofilm stability and detachment.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which of the following best explains how phosphate starvation triggers biofilm detachment in wild-type Pseudomonas aeruginosa?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) Phosphate starvation increases c-di-GMP, causing LapG to remain inactive and biofilms to persist.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) Phosphate starvation upregulates the Pho regulon, leading to RapA-mediated c-di-GMP degradation and LapG activation for biofilm detachment.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) Phosphate starvation induces expression of outer membrane markers that disrupt biofilm structure directly.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) Phosphate starvation stimulates LapD to constitutively bind LapG, preventing LapA cleavage and promoting detachment.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) Phosphate starvation causes all proteins to relocalize to the inner membrane, triggering biofilm dispersal.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Phosphate starvation reduces all detergent activity, resulting in spontaneous LapA cleavage and biofilm loss.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) Phosphate starvation stabilizes LapA, preventing any changes in biofilm structure.
