[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m Human cytomegalovirus (HCMV) infection triggers a range of host DNA damage response mechanisms. The virus interacts with key cellular proteins such as ATM, ATR, Chk2, Chk1, p53, and Rad51 to modulate the environment for its own replication.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which of the following accurately describes the behavior of host DNA repair and checkpoint proteins during late-stage HCMV infection in human fibroblasts?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) ATM and its phosphorylated form are activated and required for efficient viral replication.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) ATR is completely sequestered within viral replication centers, initiating a full replication stress response.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) p53 is degraded during infection, reducing its influence on viral replication and gene expression.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) Rad51 is sequestered to viral genomes, facilitating homologous recombination-based repair of viral DNA.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) ATM and Chk2 are partially sequestered in replication centers, but ATM is not necessary for HCMV infection progression.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Chk1 and ATR are excluded from replication centers and remain inactive throughout late-stage infection.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) p53 levels decline sharply during late infection, disrupting viral production in host cells.
