[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m Transition metal doping in anatase TiO₂ significantly influences its structural, electronic, and magnetic properties, often leading to defect formation and altered charge compensation mechanisms. Understanding how dopants like Cu²⁺ interact with the TiO₂ lattice and how associated defects affect local structure is crucial for tuning material performance.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m When Cu²⁺ substitutes for Ti⁴⁺ in anatase TiO₂, which process most effectively compensates for the resulting charge imbalance, and what is the characteristic structural consequence near the Cu site?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) Electron transfer from neighboring Ti ions, leading to shortened all Cu-O bonds to below 1.90 Å   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) Formation of a titanium interstitial, causing expansion of the entire lattice by 0.5 Å   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) Introduction of an oxygen vacancy near the Cu dopant, resulting in five-fold coordination of Cu and Ti and outward movement of Ti ions with shortened opposite metal-oxygen bonds   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) Reduction of the Cu ion to the Cu⁺ state, with no change in local coordination or bond distances   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) Migration of Cu to an interstitial site, generating equatorial bond lengths of 2.5 Å   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Formation of a titanium vacancy, leading to collapse of the local lattice around the defect   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) Substitution of a neighboring oxygen with sulfur, causing elongation of all Ti-O bonds beyond 2.5 Å
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Please provide your reasoning within <think></think> tags and the final answer within a \boxed{} block. The value inside the \boxed{} block should only be the number of the correct option. So the final answer is just \boxed{3}.
