[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m Global transcriptional regulators such as ArcA, Crp, and EtrA play vital roles in controlling aerobic and anaerobic respiration in Gram-negative bacteria. The construction and analysis of single, double, and triple gene deletion mutants enable the investigation of their individual and collective impacts on cellular processes like electron transport and cytochrome biosynthesis.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which of the following experimental findings most strongly indicates that ArcA, Crp, and EtrA have overlapping roles in regulating c-type cytochrome biosynthesis in Shewanella oneidensis?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) Single, double, and triple mutants all show significantly reduced heme c levels compared to wild-type under both aerobic and anaerobic conditions.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) Western blotting fails to detect ArcA protein only in the arcA deletion mutant.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) The parental strain MR-1R exhibits altered menaquinone levels due to rifampin resistance.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) A LacZ reporter system is introduced to monitor transcriptional regulation in bacteria lacking native beta-galactosidase.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) Only the triple mutant shows any change in c-type cytochrome levels, while single and double mutants remain unaffected.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Mutants demonstrate normal growth on aerobic media but fail to grow anaerobically.   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) Cellular heme c concentrations are higher under anaerobic conditions in wild-type but not in mutants.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
