[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m Soil-dwelling Streptomycete bacteria are well known for producing antibiotics and playing a key role in shaping microbial community dynamics through both chemical inhibition and resistance mechanisms. Comparing interactions among isolates from the same and different locations can shed light on local adaptation and coevolutionary processes.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which experimental approach most directly tests whether close spatial proximity among Streptomycete isolates promotes the evolution of stronger antibiotic inhibition or resistance traits?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) Comparing antibiotic inhibition between isolates from nutrient-rich and nutrient-poor soils   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) Assessing inhibitory and resistance phenotypes in isolates from both temperate and tropical habitats   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) Characterizing genetic diversity among isolates using 16S rRNA sequencing   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) Measuring inhibitory and resistance traits in all possible pairwise combinations of sympatric and allopatric isolates   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) Analyzing the relationship between antibiotic production and soil pH variation   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Recording morphological differences among isolates from various core samples   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) Evaluating the ability of isolates to degrade complex soil polymers
