[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m [34mℹ[0m [1m[34m[QUESTION][0m Mathematical models are frequently used to understand the ecological dynamics of the human microbiome, especially in response to disturbances such as antibiotics. A shift from traditional multi-parameter species interaction models toward diversity-based modeling has provided new insights into long-term effects on microbial communities.
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m Which feature distinguishes a diversity-centric mathematical model of microbiome response to antibiotics from traditional Lotka-Volterra models, and directly enables detection of long-term state transitions following short antibiotic exposure?
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 1) Reliance on competitive exclusion as the primary mechanism of community change   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 2) Use of species abundance alone without considering genetic relatedness   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 3) Modeling pairwise interactions among all microbial species   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 4) Incorporation of phylogenetic diversity as a key dynamic metric   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 5) Assumption that the microbiome always rapidly returns to its original state   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 6) Exclusive use of shotgun metagenomics data   
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 7) Neglect of temporal changes in microbial diversity
[36m(main_task pid=1434988)[0m 
